{"id": "live-170", "title": "LRK, LLC v. City of St. George (Kleinpeter Tract Downzoning Challenge — TRO Granted)", "category": "zoning-land-use", "status": "pending", "date": "2026-07-13", "jurisdiction": "19th Judicial District Court, East Baton Rouge Parish, Louisiana", "state": "LA", "amount": "~60-acre commercially zoned parcel; declaratory and injunctive relief sought", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.theadvocate.com/baton_rouge/news/courts/st-george-rezoning-lawsuit/article_b2538455-124f-4683-bffe-323678f2c4a3.html", "summary": "A 19th Judicial District Court judge signed a temporary restraining order in July 2026 blocking the City of St. George, Louisiana from adopting or enforcing a proposed ordinance that would downzone nearly 60 acres of commercially zoned land at the Pecue Lane/I-10 interchange owned by an entity tied to the Kleinpeter family. Plaintiff LRK, LLC's June 8, 2026 suit also asks the court to declare the city's own legal incorporation date, a question tied to the scope of St. George's zoning and ordinance-making authority.", "significance": "Illustrates the risk a wholesale zoning-code consolidation poses to parcels holding stable, decades-old commercial entitlements, and shows a TRO can freeze a pending rezoning ordinance before it takes effect. The case's added challenge to the city's own incorporation date is a reminder that a young or recently reorganized municipality's zoning authority can itself become a point of legal attack.", "judge": null, "tags": ["zoning", "louisiana", "downzoning", "temporary restraining order", "incorporation dispute"]}
